window.EXAM=[{"id":"u1","n":1,"t":"Lifestyles","a":["hectic","mindful","priority","deliberately","overwhelmed","cut back","clutter","savour","commitment","recharge"],"b":["screen time","notification","distraction","addictive","willpower","boundary","constantly","disconnect","productivity","urge","scroll","habit"],"box":["recharge","hectic","deliberately","overwhelmed","savour","cut back","priorities","mindful","clutter","commitment"],"gap":[["After a busy month, I need a holiday to ___.","recharge"],["My life has been really ___ since I started two jobs.","hectic"],["She ___ turned off notifications to concentrate.","deliberately"],["Too many emails made him feel ___.","overwhelmed"],["I try to ___ every bite of a good meal.","savour"],["We should ___ on plastic to help the planet.","cut back"],["Health and sleep are my top ___.","priorities"],["Being ___ means noticing the present moment.","mindful"],["A tidy room has less ___ and feels calmer.","clutter"],["Going to the gym three times a week is a big ___.","commitment"]],"rt":"The Art of Slowing Down","rp":["Modern life can feel incredibly hectic. Many people wake up to a long list of tasks, rush through the day, and fall into bed exhausted. Between work, study, social activities, and constant notifications, there is often very little time left to simply pause. People may feel that they always need to be doing something, even during their free time. In recent years, however, a growing number of people have decided to cut back on this fast pace. They are part of what is known as the \"slow living\" movement.","Slow living does not mean being lazy or doing everything slowly. Instead, it means being more mindful about how we spend our time and energy. People who choose this lifestyle try to focus on their real priorities rather than filling every moment with activity. They may think more carefully about which activities are important to them and which ones simply make their days busier. They deliberately make time to savour simple pleasures, such as cooking a meal, reading a book, or talking with a friend. For some people, this can also mean enjoying a quiet morning, taking a walk, or spending an evening without a busy schedule.","Anna, a graphic designer from Berlin, felt completely overwhelmed by her schedule two years ago. She often felt that one busy day simply followed another, leaving her with little energy for herself. \"I was always tired and never happy,\" she explains. \"So, I decided to change. I reduced my working hours and started saying no to commitments I didn't enjoy.\" Now she has more time to recharge and feels much healthier. She has also discovered that having more free time allows her to enjoy ordinary parts of her day that she used to overlook.","Not everyone finds it easy. Marco, a student, admits that slowing down is difficult when your phone is full of messages and your calendar is cluttered with deadlines. Like many students, he has to balance different responsibilities and often feels pressure to keep up with everything. \"The idea is wonderful,\" he says, \"but in reality, university life doesn't always allow it.\" Although he likes the idea of having a calmer lifestyle, he knows that changing his routine is not always simple.","Experts say that even small changes can help. Turning off your phone during meals, or spending ten minutes outside, can create a better balance. Other simple habits, such as taking short breaks or leaving some free time between activities, may also help people feel less rushed. The goal is not to completely change your lifestyle overnight. Instead, people can begin by making small adjustments to the way they use their time. In a world that never seems to stop, learning to slow down may be one of the most valuable skills of all."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the text?","o":["To explain the main reasons behind the increasingly fast pace of modern life","To describe a lifestyle choice that emphasizes doing less while focusing on quality","To encourage people to give up their careers and stop working completely","To examine the differences between the lifestyles of two German cities"],"a":1},{"q":"In the text, \"cut back on\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["reduce the amount of something","increase the amount of something","completely eliminate something from your life","make greater use of something"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did Anna change her lifestyle?","o":["She was unexpectedly forced to leave her job.","She decided to start a new life after moving to Berlin.","She had become increasingly exhausted and dissatisfied with her life.","She was advised by her doctor to adopt a slower lifestyle."],"a":2},{"q":"What can we understand about Marco's opinion?","o":["He is convinced that slow living is ultimately harmful.","He finds the concept appealing, although he struggles to put it into practice.","He has successfully transformed every aspect of his lifestyle.","He is largely indifferent to the idea and its potential benefits."],"a":1},{"q":"According to experts, what can help people find balance?","o":["Giving up higher education and focusing on personal interests","Avoiding daily responsibilities and spending most of the day resting","Introducing manageable changes, such as having meals without using phones","Purchasing more products in order to make everyday life more convenient"],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Anna","Marco","Experts"],"q":[["Even small changes in our daily routines can make a real difference.","Experts"],["I stopped agreeing to things that I didn't really enjoy or find meaningful.","Anna"],["Slow living is an appealing idea, but it can be difficult for students to put it into practice.","Marco"],["I now feel much healthier and have more energy than I did before.","Anna"],["Turning off your phone during meals can help create a healthier balance in everyday life.","Experts"]]},"lq":[{"q":"Who is the expert in the interview?","o":["Tom Bradley, a university student","Dr Elena Frost, a well-being coach","The radio presenter","A phone company manager"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the interview mainly about?","o":["Choosing the right phone for everyday use","Building a healthier relationship with screens","Understanding why students fail exams","Exploring how the internet has changed over time"],"a":1},{"q":"How many hours a day does the average adult spend on screens?","o":["around three","around five","around seven","around ten"],"a":2},{"q":"According to Elena, what is the real problem?","o":["Having too many apps on our devices","Checking our phones repeatedly throughout the day","Spending too much money on new technology","Depending on a fast internet connection"],"a":1},{"q":"\"I don't have the willpower to stop\" means Tom:","o":["is unsure how to change his habit.","finds it difficult to control the habit.","doesn't see any reason to change.","has already managed to break the habit."],"a":1},{"q":"What can we understand about Tom by the end?","o":["Changing his habits was too difficult for him.","Making one small change made a noticeable difference.","He remained unconvinced by Elena's advice.","He decided to stop using his phone completely."],"a":1}],"lwho":{"p":["Elena","Tom"],"q":[["Apps are designed to keep us engaged for as long as possible.","Elena"],["I find it difficult to break the habit of checking my phone.","Tom"],["Leaving your phone outside the bedroom can lead to better sleep.","Elena"],["Since I changed my screen habits, I've become more productive.","Tom"],["You don't have to cut out screens completely — the ultimate goal is to find a healthier balance.","Elena"]]},"au":"audio/u1.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"In the first paragraph, \"hectic\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["very busy and full of activity","calm and quiet","slow and boring","healthy and relaxing"],"a":0},{"q":"According to the text, what does slow living mean?","o":["Doing everything as slowly as possible","Being lazy and avoiding work","Being more mindful about how we spend our time and energy","Moving to a quiet village"],"a":2},{"q":"What did Anna do to change her life?","o":["She changed her job.","She moved to a new city.","She stopped using her phone.","She reduced her working hours and said no to some commitments."],"a":3},{"q":"Why does Marco find slowing down difficult?","o":["His phone is full of messages and his calendar is full of deadlines.","He doesn't like the idea.","He has no friends.","He works too many hours at a café."],"a":0},{"q":"According to the last paragraph, what is the goal?","o":["To change your whole lifestyle overnight","To make small adjustments to the way you use your time","To stop using technology","To work fewer days a week"],"a":1},{"q":"Which suggestion do the experts make?","o":["Spending ten minutes outside","Buying a new calendar","Working only in the morning","Changing jobs every year"],"a":0}],"lq2":[{"q":"What is the first thing Tom touches in the morning?","o":["His alarm clock","His phone","His breakfast","His laptop"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Elena advise clients to do about the bedroom?","o":["Put a television in it","Keep the phone out of the bedroom","Buy a new bed","Use a bigger phone"],"a":1},{"q":"What did Tom buy to replace his phone alarm?","o":["A traditional alarm clock","A smartwatch","A new mattress","A radio"],"a":0},{"q":"How were the first few nights without the phone for Tom?","o":["Really hard","Very easy","Boring","Exciting"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Elena say is the ultimate goal?","o":["Zero screen time","Finding a healthy balance","Selling phones","Reading more books"],"a":1},{"q":"According to Elena, why do notifications affect our focus?","o":["They make a lot of noise.","They pull our attention away from what we are doing.","They are always important.","They use too much battery."],"a":1}],"ph":[{"q":"I'd put \"no phone during meals\" _____ because it's the easiest habit.","o":["first","before","early","one"],"a":0},{"q":"I think a screen-free evening would be _____ because I use my phone to relax.","o":["harder","hardly","hardness","hardening"],"a":0},{"q":"I'm not _____ that would work for me because I need my phone for work.","o":["sure","agree","think","want"],"a":0},{"q":"We could make it easier _____ turning off non-essential notifications.","o":["by","with","for","to"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u2","n":2,"t":"Makers","a":["invention","entrepreneur","prototype","launch","affordable","innovative","determined","tackle","profit","workshop","inspire","demand"],"b":["artificial intelligence","generate","algorithm","original","imitate","replace","impressive","threat","tool","debate","imagination","skill"],"box":["innovative","prototype","tackle","entrepreneur","launch","affordable","determined","profit","workshop","invention"],"gap":[["This clever, ___ design won a prize for its originality.","innovative"],["Before selling it, she tested the first ___ at home.","prototype"],["He wants to ___ the problem of plastic in the ocean.","tackle"],["A good ___ isn't afraid to start a new business.","entrepreneur"],["They will ___ their new gadget online next week.","launch"],["The tools are cheap and ___ for everyone.","affordable"],["She was so ___ that she never gave up.","determined"],["After paying all the costs, their ___ was small.","profit"],["Students build and repair things in the school ___.","workshop"],["The smartphone is probably the most important ___ of our time.","invention"]],"rt":"The Rise of Young Makers","rp":["Not long ago, turning an idea into a real product was expensive and slow. Today, thanks to cheap technology like 3D printers, almost anyone can become a maker. Around the world, more and more young people are designing inventions in their bedrooms and garages — and some are even becoming entrepreneurs and starting their own companies. Many young makers are motivated by everyday problems rather than complicated scientific questions. They often start small, using materials that are easy to find and learning as they go. For some, the experience is more important than creating a perfect product on the first try.","Take Leila, a seventeen-year-old from Manchester. When her grandmother struggled to open jars because of arthritis, Leila designed a simple plastic tool to help. She built her first prototype using a 3D printer in her school's workshop. At first, the tool was uncomfortable to hold, so she went back to her original design and made several small changes. After many failed attempts, she finally created something that worked. \"I was so determined to solve the problem,\" she says. \"I didn't give up.\" Her affordable invention is now sold online, and the demand keeps growing.","Others focus on protecting the planet. Diego, aged nineteen, noticed how much plastic his family threw away. He decided to tackle the problem by turning old bottles into innovative furniture. He launched a small business and began by making a few pieces for people in his local community. Gradually, his products attracted attention from other customers. \"I wanted to prove that being green can also be good business,\" he explains. His products are expensive to make, so his profit is still small — but he believes it will grow.","Experts say this \"maker movement\" is changing how young people think about work. Instead of waiting for the perfect job, they create their own. This approach encourages students to take more responsibility for their own ideas. They can decide what they want to explore and learn to deal with unexpected problems along the way. Schools are helping too, by opening workshops where students can build and experiment. \"Young makers aren't afraid to fail,\" one teacher notes. \"Every mistake teaches them something.\" Their experiences can also inspire other students to turn their own ideas into projects.","Of course, not every idea becomes successful. For some young makers, the biggest reward is not becoming famous or making a lot of money. It is seeing an idea develop from something on paper into something that people can actually use. But even those who fail learn valuable skills. As Leila puts it, \"You don't need to be a genius to be a maker. You just need a problem you really want to fix — and the patience to keep trying.\""],"rq":[{"q":"What is the text mainly about?","o":["Young people who are developing their own inventions and turning them into businesses","The steps involved in setting up a successful furniture business","The reasons why 3D printers remain expensive","The historical development of modern technology"],"a":0},{"q":"In the context of the text, the word “prototype” refers to:","o":["A facility where products are manufactured on a large scale","A copy of a well-known commercial product","An initial version of a product developed for testing and improvement","A detailed plan for establishing a new business"],"a":2},{"q":"Why did Leila design her tool?","o":["She needed to create an original product for a school examination.","She wanted to generate income as quickly as possible.","She wanted to find a way to reduce the amount of plastic people use.","She wanted to make it easier for her grandmother to open jars."],"a":3},{"q":"What can we understand about Diego's business?","o":["It has already become highly profitable.","It has a meaningful purpose, although it has not generated much profit so far.","It operates exclusively through online sales.","It has failed completely despite his efforts."],"a":1},{"q":"According to the text, how are schools helping?","o":["By providing students with financial support for their projects","By helping students find employment after graduation","By providing workshops where students can create, test, and experiment with their ideas","By marketing and selling students' inventions on their behalf"],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Leila","Diego","Experts/Teacher"],"q":[["I wanted to show that being eco-friendly can be good business.","Diego"],["Young makers learn from every mistake.","Experts/Teacher"],["I refused to give up until it worked.","Leila"],["You just need a problem you really want to fix.","Leila"],["The maker movement is changing how young people see work.","Experts/Teacher"]]},"lq":[{"q":"What is the main question of the discussion?","o":["How to become a successful artist","How to build a working algorithm","Whether machines can truly be creative","Why paintings are expensive"],"a":2},{"q":"How does Dr Chen say AI produces art?","o":["It studies millions of existing works and makes something similar.","It copies the style of one famous painting.","It asks human artists for help.","It uses its own imagination."],"a":0},{"q":"What was Sara's first reaction to AI art?","o":["She was excited about the new technology.","She thought the art looked ugly.","She didn't really care about it.","She was worried it might end human art."],"a":3},{"q":"\"imitate\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["improve something","copy something closely","destroy something","sell something"],"a":1},{"q":"What do both guests seem to believe by the end?","o":["AI will replace all human artists.","AI should be banned from art.","Humans and AI can work well together.","Art is no longer important."],"a":2}],"lwho":{"p":["Sara","Dr Chen"],"q":[["AI is clever imitation, but it has no feelings or story.","Dr Chen"],["The final emotion in my art still comes from me.","Sara"],["New technology usually creates new opportunities too.","Dr Chen"],["Artists who succeed will learn to use these tools.","Sara"],["AI will change how designers work, not replace them.","Dr Chen"]]},"au":"audio/u2.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"In the text, \"affordable\" means:","o":["cheap enough for many people to buy","very beautiful","difficult to make","easy to use"],"a":0},{"q":"Why did Leila make changes to her first tool?","o":["It was too expensive.","It was uncomfortable to hold.","It was too large for her school.","Her grandmother didn't like the colour."],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Diego's profit still small?","o":["He sells too few products.","His products are expensive to make.","He gives them away.","His customers are unhappy."],"a":1},{"q":"Where did Diego begin selling his furniture?","o":["On the internet","In a large shop","In his local community","At his school"],"a":2},{"q":"What does the teacher's comment about mistakes suggest?","o":["Young makers learn from failure.","Young makers should not make mistakes.","Mistakes are expensive.","Teachers should fix every mistake."],"a":0},{"q":"What is the biggest reward for some young makers?","o":["Becoming famous","Making a lot of money","Seeing their idea become something people can use","Winning a prize at school"],"a":2}],"lq2":[{"q":"How long did the AI take to produce the painting Sara watched?","o":["About 30 seconds","About 30 minutes","About a week","About three hours"],"a":0},{"q":"What did Sara feel was missing in the AI images?","o":["Colour","Real meaning","Size","Detail"],"a":1},{"q":"How could a designer use AI, according to Dr Chen?","o":["To create many rough ideas quickly","To replace all other designers","To avoid making any decisions","To print final artworks"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Sara use AI in her own work?","o":["To finish her paintings","To generate rough ideas when she feels stuck","To sell her art","To write emails"],"a":1},{"q":"Which skills does Dr Chen think will become more valuable?","o":["Imagination and storytelling","Typing and counting","Driving and cooking","Speed and strength"],"a":0},{"q":"What does \"The camera didn't kill painting\" mean?","o":["New technology can change an art without ending it.","Painting was never popular.","Cameras are cheaper than paintings.","Painters hate cameras."],"a":0}],"ph":[{"q":"Our invention _____ the problem of plastic waste.","o":["tackles","makes","builds","tries"],"a":0},{"q":"AI could help us _____ giving us new ideas.","o":["by","for","at","in"],"a":0},{"q":"We could use AI _____ create rough sketches.","o":["to","by","for","at"],"a":0},{"q":"I don't think AI _____ make the final decision.","o":["should","to","is","for"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u3","n":3,"t":"Challenges","a":["resilience","setback","overcome","failure","bounce back","cope","persevere","mindset","obstacle","give up","confidence","motivation"],"b":["adapt","adjust","challenge","routine","homesick","unfamiliar","gradually","support","comfort zone","struggle","get used to","independent"],"box":["setback","mindset","overcome","persevere","give up","obstacle","resilience","cope","failure","bounce back"],"gap":[["Losing all his money was a huge ___, but he didn't quit.","setback"],["A positive ___ helps you see problems as lessons.","mindset"],["She managed to ___ her fear and speak on stage.","overcome"],["If you ___, you'll finish the project eventually.","persevere"],["Never just ___ because something is hard.","give up"],["The main ___ to her success was a lack of time.","obstacle"],["People with ___ recover quickly from difficulties.","resilience"],["He learned to ___ with pressure by taking breaks.","cope"],["One ___ is not the end of the world.","failure"],["Good athletes always ___ after a bad game.","bounce back"]],"rt":"Bouncing Back: The Power of Resilience","rp":["Everyone experiences failure at some point. We fail a test, lose a game, or make a mistake at work. What matters, psychologists say, is not the failure itself but how we respond to it. This ability to recover from difficulties is called resilience — and the good news is that it can be learned. People can face very different kinds of difficulties in their personal, academic, or professional lives. What feels like a small problem to one person may be much more difficult for another.","Consider Maya, a long-distance runner. Two years ago, a serious injury stopped her from competing, and doctors said she might never run again. \"It was the biggest setback of my life,\" she remembers. \"For weeks I wanted to give up.\" But instead of quitting, she slowly rebuilt her strength and worked hard to overcome her injury. Her experience changed the way she thought about her goals, and she began to see progress differently. Last spring, she finished her first marathon since the injury. \"The pain taught me patience,\" she says.","Failure can also lead to success in business. When Tom opened his first café, it closed within a year. He had lost his savings and felt like a complete failure. However, he refused to let one mistake define him. The experience stayed with him for a long time, and he thought carefully about what he had learned from it. He studied what went wrong, changed his plan, and opened a second café. Today it is one of the most popular in his city. \"Every obstacle taught me something useful,\" he explains. His experience also gave him the motivation to try again.","Experts believe that a positive mindset is the key. People with resilience don't see failure as the end; they see it as a lesson. They understand that difficult experiences can affect their confidence and emotions, but they try to keep these experiences in perspective. They also know how to cope with stress by asking for help, staying hopeful, and focusing on small steps forward. People who persevere through difficult periods are often better prepared to bounce back from future challenges.","Building resilience takes time and practice. It can develop gradually as people experience challenges and learn more about how they respond to them. The next time you face a difficult moment, remember Maya and Tom. Success is rarely a straight line. Often, the people who achieve the most are simply the ones who refused to give up trying."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the writer's main message?","o":["Failure is something that should be avoided at all costs.","How we respond to failure matters more than the failure itself","Resilience is a quality that can only be developed by professional athletes.","Taking up running is one of the best ways to maintain good health."],"a":1},{"q":"In the context of the text, the word “setback” is closest in meaning to:","o":["an unexpected success or achievement","a challenging event in which people compete against each other","a difficulty that prevents or delays further progress","a choice made after carefully considering different possibilities"],"a":2},{"q":"What did Maya do after her injury?","o":["She decided to give up running permanently after her injury.","She decided to study medicine and become a doctor.","She changed careers and opened a café instead.","She gradually regained her strength and eventually completed a marathon."],"a":3},{"q":"What can we understand about Tom?","o":["He had never experienced failure or made any significant mistakes.","He learned from his initial failure and was willing to make another attempt.","He failed to keep his second café open and eventually closed it as well.","He had little or no interest in running a business in the first place."],"a":1},{"q":"According to experts, how do resilient people cope with stress?","o":["Ignoring their difficulties until they eventually disappear","Giving up when a situation becomes too challenging to handle","Seeking support from others and focusing on manageable steps","Dealing with their problems independently without relying on others"],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Maya","Tom","Experts"],"q":[["Difficulty taught me to be patient.","Maya"],["Resilient people treat failure as a lesson, not an ending.","Experts"],["I studied my mistake and changed my plan.","Tom"],["Doctors thought I might never do my sport again.","Maya"],["Recovering from problems is a skill you can learn.","Experts"]]},"lq":[{"q":"Where did Aylin move?","o":["To Canada (from Turkey)","To Turkey (from Canada)","To a huge city (from a tiny village)","To a tiny village (from a huge city)"],"a":0},{"q":"Where did Marco move?","o":["To Canada (from Turkey)","To another country","To a huge city (from a tiny village)","To a tiny village (from a huge city)"],"a":2},{"q":"What are Aylin and Marco mainly talking about?","o":["How they adapted to a major change","The subjects they enjoy most","Ways to travel on a small budget","Their experiences with part-time jobs"],"a":0},{"q":"How did Aylin start to make friends?","o":["By keeping in touch with her family","By spending more time in her room","By deciding to move back home","By joining a photography club"],"a":3},{"q":"What helped Marco feel more in control?","o":["Taking the bus to school every day","Creating a regular daily routine","Moving back to his home village","Spending less time with other people"],"a":1},{"q":"\"homesick\" means:","o":["feeling tired after a long journey","feeling ill while travelling somewhere","feeling sad because you are away from home","feeling excited about living ina new place"],"a":2},{"q":"What do both students agree about?","o":["Itis better to deal with problems on your own.","Having support from others can make change easier.","Moving to a new place is usually a bad idea.","Joining clubs is not helpful when things change."],"a":1}],"lwho":{"p":["Aylin","Marco"],"q":[["Moving to another country left me feeling homesick.","Aylin"],["Following a daily routine gave me a stronger sense of control.","Marco"],["Joining a club turned out to be a turning point for me.","Aylin"],["Being too shy to ask questions made the situation more difficult.","Marco"],["The move helped me become much more independent.","Marco"]]},"au":"audio/u3.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"According to the text, what is resilience?","o":["The ability to recover from difficulties","The ability to run long distances","A type of business skill","A way of avoiding failure"],"a":0},{"q":"What does the writer say about problems?","o":["Everyone finds the same things difficult.","What seems small to one person may be very difficult for another.","Small problems are never important.","Only adults have serious problems."],"a":1},{"q":"What did Maya learn from her pain?","o":["To be patient","To give up","To study medicine","To run faster"],"a":0},{"q":"What happened to Tom's first café?","o":["It became very popular.","It closed within a year.","It was sold to a friend.","It moved to a new city."],"a":1},{"q":"What did Tom do after his first café closed?","o":["He gave up business.","He opened a second café.","He moved abroad.","He became a runner."],"a":1},{"q":"In the text, \"persevere\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["continue trying although it is difficult","stop doing something","change your plan","ask for help"],"a":0}],"lq2":[{"q":"How often did Aylin call her family in her first month?","o":["Every day","Once a week","Once a month","Never"],"a":0},{"q":"What was the turning point for Aylin?","o":["Moving back home","Joining a photography club","Calling her teacher","Changing her room"],"a":1},{"q":"Where did Marco grow up?","o":["In a huge city","In Canada","In a tiny village","In a small town by the sea"],"a":2},{"q":"What made even taking the bus stressful for Marco?","o":["Everything was fast and noisy.","The buses were always late.","He didn't have money.","He was afraid of heights."],"a":0},{"q":"Who became a great source of support for Marco?","o":["His teacher","His roommate","His brother","His boss"],"a":1},{"q":"What advice does Aylin give at the start of her story?","o":["Stay in your room","Step out of your comfort zone","Always call home","Avoid new people"],"a":1}],"ph":[{"q":"Our first _____ is to join a club.","o":["tip","tipping","tips of","tipped"],"a":0},{"q":"They should _____ to ask for help early.","o":["try","trying","tried","tries"],"a":0},{"q":"This can help them adjust _____ they will meet new people.","o":["because","but","although","unless"],"a":0},{"q":"We think this is more important _____ making a perfect plan.","o":["than","that","then","as"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u4","n":4,"t":"Science","a":["device","monitor","sensor","data","detect","accurate","diagnose","treatment","prevent","reliable","breakthrough","wearable"],"b":["renewable","fossil fuels","emissions","sustainable","solar power","efficient","resource","reduce","pollution","alternative","invest","carbon footprint"],"box":["breakthrough","monitor","data","sensor","diagnose","accurate","detect","treatment","prevent","device"],"gap":[["A new medical ___ could change how we treat illness.","breakthrough"],["My watch can ___ my heart rate 24 hours a day.","monitor"],["The app collects a lot of personal ___.","data"],["A tiny ___ inside the watch measures movement.","sensor"],["Doctors could not ___ the illness until it was serious.","diagnose"],["Good technology should be exact and ___.","accurate"],["This tool can ___ problems you cannot feel.","detect"],["Early care means better ___ for patients.","treatment"],["Healthy habits can ___ many diseases.","prevent"],["A smartphone is the most common ___ in the world.","device"]],"rt":"Health at Your Wrist","rp":["A few years ago, a watch could only tell you the time. Today, many watches can do much more. Modern wearable devices can monitor your heart rate, count your steps, check your sleep, and even measure the oxygen in your blood. For millions of people, health information is now available right on their wrist. People use these devices for different reasons. Some want to become more active, while others simply enjoy having information about their daily habits. As the technology becomes more common, wearable technology is becoming a familiar part of everyday life.","These devices use tiny sensors to collect data about the body. Some can detect problems that the wearer cannot feel. Take David, a 45-year-old teacher. One evening, his smartwatch warned him that his heart rhythm was unusual. At first, he ignored it, but the warning continued. When he finally visited a hospital, doctors discovered a serious heart condition. \"That little device probably saved my life,\" he says. The experience changed the way David thought about the information on his wrist. He had never expected a small device to provide information that could be useful in such an important situation.","Doctors are becoming more interested in this technology too. Dr Silva, a heart specialist, explains that wearables allow patients to be monitored at home instead of in hospital. \"We can now collect information over weeks, not just during a short visit,\" she says. \"This helps us diagnose problems earlier and choose better treatment.\" For doctors, having access to information collected during normal daily life can provide a different picture from what they see during a single appointment. This may be especially useful when symptoms do not appear every day.","However, experts also warn that the technology is not perfect. The results are not always accurate, and a watch is not a replacement for a real doctor. Some people worry about privacy, because these devices collect huge amounts of personal data. \"Wearables are a useful tool,\" one researcher notes, \"but they should support medical care, not replace it.\" Users also need to understand that different devices may collect and present information in different ways. Learning how to use the technology correctly is therefore becoming an important part of using wearables responsibly.","Scientists believe this is only the beginning. In the future, wearable devices may be able to detect illnesses even earlier and help prevent them. This kind of breakthrough could change medicine forever. Researchers are also exploring new ways to make these devices smaller, more comfortable, and easier to use. As the technology develops, the amount of information collected from the human body may continue to grow. The dream is simple: to keep people healthy before they ever become sick."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the article mainly about?","o":["The most affordable ways to purchase wearable technology","The ways in which wearable devices can contribute to healthcare and medical treatment","The reasons why doctors may no longer be necessary in the future","The historical development of clocks and timekeeping devices"],"a":1},{"q":"In the context of the article, the word “monitor” is closest in meaning to:","o":["to offer something for sale","to keep something out of sight or prevent it from being noticed","to damage something so that it no longer works","to observe and check something regularly over a period of time"],"a":3},{"q":"What did David's smartwatch do?","o":["It only kept track of the number of steps he took each day.","It detected an unusual heart rhythm and alerted him to a possible health problem.","It automatically contacted a doctor on his behalf.","It suddenly stopped functioning while he was wearing it."],"a":1},{"q":"Why are doctors like Dr Silva interested in wearables?","o":["They believe wearable technology could eventually eliminate the need to see patients.","They are interested in wearables mainly because they consider smartwatches fashionable.","Wearables can provide health data over extended periods, potentially allowing doctors to identify problems at an earlier stage.","They believe wearable devices could encourage patients to remain in hospital for longer"],"a":2},{"q":"What warning do experts give?","o":["The information provided by wearable devices is unreliable.","The results are not always accurate and shouldn't replace a doctor.","People should avoid using wearable devices for health-related purposes altogether.","Wearable devices are too cheap to provide trustworthy health information."],"a":1}],"who":{"p":["David","Dr Silva","Experts/Researcher"],"q":[["This kind of device probably saved my life.","David"],["Wearables should support medical care, not replace it.","Experts/Researcher"],["We can now collect data over weeks, not just one visit.","Dr Silva"],["At first, I ignored the warning.","David"],["People should be careful about their personal data.","Experts/Researcher"]]},"lq":[{"q":"Who is more optimistic about renewable energy at the start of the discussion?","o":["Paul","Dr Osei","Both are equally optimistic","Neither of them"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the main question of the discussion?","o":["How to build a modern smartphone","Replacing fossil fuels with renewable energy","Ways to save money while travelling","Which country is the biggest"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Dr Osei say about solar power today?","o":["It is now one of the cheapest energy sources.","It is still too expensive for most people.","It is not reliable enough to use widely.","It is banned in most countries."],"a":0},{"q":"What is Paul's main concern?","o":["That clean energy systems are unattractive","The high cost of making the change","That solar panels may be dangerous","That people are not interested in clean energy"],"a":1},{"q":"\"invest\" means:","o":["TOospend money without a clear purpose","To put money in now to gain something later","To take money from someone illegally","To keep money somewhere for a long time"],"a":1},{"q":"What do both guests finally agree on?","o":["Renewable energy will play an important role in the future.","Fossil fuels are still the better choice.","There is no practical solution to the problem.","There is little people can do to make a difference."],"a":0}],"lwho":{"p":["Dr Osei","Paul"],"q":[["Solar power has become one of the cheapest energy sources.","Dr Osei"],["On a cloudy, calm day, we still need backup power.","Paul"],["Not changing will be even more expensive in the end.","Dr Osei"],["Building all the new equipment costs a great deal of money.","Paul"],["Governments investing properly could make change happen faster.","Dr Osei"]]},"au":"audio/u4.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"Which of these can modern wearable devices do, according to the text?","o":["Take photographs of the heart","Cook healthy meals","Monitor your heart rate and count your steps","Replace doctors completely"],"a":2},{"q":"How did David react to the first warning from his smartwatch?","o":["He called an ambulance.","He ignored it at first.","He took the watch off.","He went to hospital immediately."],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Dr Silva think wearables are useful?","o":["They are cheaper than hospitals.","They let doctors collect information over weeks.","They make appointments unnecessary.","They never make mistakes."],"a":1},{"q":"Why are some people worried about wearables?","o":["The devices are too heavy.","The devices collect huge amounts of personal data.","The devices are very expensive.","The devices only work in hospitals."],"a":1},{"q":"In the last paragraph, \"breakthrough\" is closest in meaning to:","o":["an important new discovery","a small problem","a type of medicine","a daily habit"],"a":0},{"q":"What is the dream of scientists mentioned in the text?","o":["To keep people healthy before they become sick","To close all hospitals","To make watches cheaper","To replace sensors with doctors"],"a":0}],"lq2":[{"q":"How long ago, according to Dr Osei, was solar power expensive and rare?","o":["10 years","30 years","50 years","2 years"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Paul say we need until energy can be stored cheaply?","o":["More fossil fuels only","Other sources as backup","More wind farms in cities","Smaller batteries"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Dr Osei say fossil fuels create?","o":["Huge amounts of emissions that harm the planet","Cheap electricity for everyone","More jobs","Cleaner air"],"a":0},{"q":"Why is the change a real challenge for poorer countries?","o":["Someone has to invest money now, although the benefits come later.","They have too much sun.","They don't need electricity.","They have no governments."],"a":0},{"q":"Why does Dr Osei say that not changing is expensive?","o":["Pollution causes health problems and climate change causes floods and droughts.","Solar panels are very cheap.","Fossil fuels will become free.","Batteries are too small."],"a":0},{"q":"Which of these can ordinary people do, according to the guests?","o":["Choose green energy suppliers","Build their own power stations","Stop using electricity","Buy bigger cars"],"a":0}],"ph":[{"q":"I wouldn't _____ on the smartwatch because it isn't always accurate.","o":["rely","relay","relate","remind"],"a":0},{"q":"I'd check the information _____ before trusting it.","o":["first","firstly of","one","early of"],"a":0},{"q":"One major _____ is that electric cars produce no emissions.","o":["advantage","advantages is","advance","advertise"],"a":0},{"q":"I'd probably buy a cheaper device _____ I don't need extra features.","o":["because","although","unless","so that"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u5","n":5,"t":"Explore","a":["destination","authentic","overcrowded","impact","preserve","resident","remote","souvenir","respect","thoughtful","consequence","mindset"],"b":["solo","adventure","freedom","spontaneous","nervous","accommodation","budget","get around","rewarding","stranger","journey","broaden your horizons"],"box":["overcrowded","preserve","impact","remote","thoughtful","souvenir","destination","residents","respect","authentic"],"gap":[["The beach was so ___ that we couldn't find anywhere to sit.","overcrowded"],["Buying local products helps ___ traditional skills.","preserve"],["Tourism can have a huge ___ on nature and on prices.","impact"],["They travelled to a ___ village that few tourists ever reach.","remote"],["A ___ traveller thinks about the effect of their choices.","thoughtful"],["I bought a small ___ to remember the trip.","souvenir"],["Barcelona is a world-famous holiday ___.","destination"],["Local ___ sometimes struggle to enjoy their own city.","residents"],["We should always ___ the customs of the places we visit.","respect"],["She wanted a real, ___ experience, not a tourist show.","authentic"]],"rt":"Travel That Gives Back: The Quiet Rise of Responsible Tourism","rp":["Every summer, millions of people head for the same famous destinations. Photographs on social media persuade thousands to book the same trip, resulting in a problem called \"overtourism\". In popular places, streets become overcrowded, house prices rise, and the beauty that first attracted visitors begins to disappear. As more people travel around the world, many destinations are finding it difficult to manage the growing number of visitors. The problem is not simply the number of tourists, but also the way they travel and the pressure their choices can place on a destination.","In recent years, however, a quieter style of travel has been growing. Called responsible, or sustainable, tourism, its central idea is simple: to explore the world without damaging it. Rather than treating a country as a backdrop for photographs, responsible travellers try to protect the environment and support local communities. This approach encourages visitors to think about the effect of their choices before and during a trip. It also asks travellers to see themselves as guests rather than simply consumers, and to consider how their presence affects the places they visit.","Emma, a young traveller from Ireland, shows how attitudes are changing. A few years ago, she used to rush from one famous landmark to the next, staying just long enough to take a picture. Today, she seeks out smaller, remote villages. \"I want an authentic experience,\" she explains. \"I stay in family-run guesthouses and eat food cooked by local people.\" For Emma, this new approach has also changed the way she plans her trips. She now spends more time researching places before she visits them and chooses accommodation based on what kind of experience she wants.","Local businesses also benefit. Mr. Costa, who runs a small guesthouse in northern Portugal, says these travellers stay longer, eat in village cafés, and hire local guides. This supports local families and helps preserve their way of life. He believes that visitors can have a positive role when they spend their money in ways that connect them with the local community. For small businesses, these choices can make tourism feel more personal and meaningful.","Experts argue that small decisions—like choosing trains over planes or buying directly from local artists—can reduce negative impacts. \"You don't have to stop travelling,\" one researcher points out. \"The goal is simply to travel a little more thoughtfully than in the past.\" There is no single perfect way to travel responsibly, and different destinations may have different needs. What matters is becoming more aware of the choices we make and their possible consequences. Responsible tourism is ultimately about a change in mindset and genuine respect for local communities."],"rq":[{"q":"What is the writer's main purpose?","o":["To discourage people from travelling abroad altogether","To describe a more thoughtful style of travel that protects places and communities","To promote small villages in Portugal and Ireland as tourist destinations","To explain how tourists can take more impressive holiday photographs"],"a":1},{"q":"According to the text, \"overtourism\" leads to all of the following EXCEPT:","o":["Local residents struggling to move freely through their neighborhoods","Housing becoming increasingly unaffordable for ordinary families","The gradual loss of the beauty of the places that attract tourists","A growing number of employment opportunities for local tour guides"],"a":3},{"q":"What can we infer about how Emma used to travel?","o":["She avoided taking photographs at popular tourist attractions.","She focused on quickly seeing famous sights rather than understanding places.","She preferred staying in expensive hotels rather than exploring local communities.","She was unwilling to travel to countries outside her own."],"a":1},{"q":"The phrase \"treating a country as a backdrop for photographs\" implies that some tourists:","o":["regard unfamiliar places as potentially dangerous and avoid exploring them","view destinations mainly as attractive settings for photographs rather than places to understand and experience","choose destinations primarily because they offer luxurious and expensive experiences","are more interested in using foreign destinations for business purposes"],"a":1},{"q":"Which sentence best expresses the researcher's opinion?","o":["Travellers should feel guilty whenever they choose to take a holiday.","International travel should be significantly reduced because of its environmental impact.","People can continue travelling as long as they make more thoughtful and responsible choices.","Travelling by train is the only acceptable option for environmentally responsible tourists."],"a":2}],"who":{"p":["Emma","Mr Costa","Researcher/Experts","Critics"],"q":[["Some people use \"responsible tourism\" just to feel good about themselves.","Critics"],["You see a completely different side of a country when you slow down.","Emma"],["Visitors now buy bread from the baker and hire local guides.","Mr Costa"],["The aim isn't to feel guilty — it's to travel with greater awareness and thoughtfulness.","Researcher/Experts"],["This way of travelling offers a richer experience for visitors while benefiting the local community.","Emma"]]},"lq":[{"q":"What is the main purpose of the radio segment?","o":["To warn listeners that solo travel is dangerous","To share two travellers' honest experiences of going alone","To advertise a particular travel company","To compare the prices of different countries"],"a":1},{"q":"How did Priya feel before her first solo trip?","o":["Confident and relaxed","Angry","Bored","Terrified"],"a":3},{"q":"What is Robert's attitude towards travelling alone at his age?","o":["He finds it embarrassing and tries to avoid it.","He thinks it is strange but does it because he has no choice.","He loves it and feels it has given him adventure again.","He does it mainly because he wants to save money."],"a":2},{"q":"Why does Robert mention staying in small guesthouses?","o":["Because they are always the cheapest option","Because he meets other travellers there, so loneliness never lasts long","Because he doesn't like staying in large hotels.","Because they are close to the mountains"],"a":1},{"q":"When Priya says every problem she solved \"made me more confident\", she means that:","o":["travelling alone was simply too difficult for her","facing and solving difficulties increased her self-belief","she never actually had any problems","she paid other people to solve her problems"],"a":1}],"lwho":{"p":["Priya","Robert"],"q":[["I'd never even eaten in a restaurant alone before my trip.","Priya"],["Some people think it's strange to travel solo at my age.","Robert"],["I've learned to enjoy my own company.","Robert"],["Solving each problem made me more confident.","Priya"],["Start small and build up — you're braver than you think.","Priya"]]},"au":"audio/u5.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"What is \"overtourism\"?","o":["Too many tourists in popular places, causing problems","Travelling abroad for work","A type of cheap holiday","Visiting small villages"],"a":0},{"q":"What is the central idea of responsible tourism?","o":["To travel less","To explore the world without damaging it","To visit only famous places","To spend as much money as possible"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Emma do now when she travels?","o":["She rushes between famous landmarks.","She stays in luxury hotels.","She stays in family-run guesthouses in smaller villages.","She travels only by plane."],"a":2},{"q":"How does Mr Costa say responsible visitors help local people?","o":["They stay longer, eat in village cafés and hire local guides.","They bring their own food.","They pay for new buildings.","They take many photographs."],"a":0},{"q":"In Emma's words, \"authentic\" means:","o":["real and true, not made for tourists","very expensive","fast and comfortable","well known"],"a":0},{"q":"Which small decision do experts mention?","o":["Choosing trains over planes","Staying at home","Travelling only in summer","Taking more photographs"],"a":0}],"lq2":[{"q":"How long was Priya's solo trip in Southeast Asia?","o":["Two weeks","Two months","Two years","Two days"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Priya like most about travelling alone?","o":["The low cost","The freedom to do exactly what she wants","The comfortable hotels","The guided tours"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Robert travel alone?","o":["His friends are too busy.","He is retired and has travelled alone since his wife passed away.","It is cheaper.","His doctor told him to."],"a":1},{"q":"Why is solo travel more expensive, according to Priya?","o":["Flights cost more.","You can't share the cost of a room.","Food is more expensive.","Guides are required."],"a":1},{"q":"How does Priya try to stay safe?","o":["She tells her family her plans and trusts her instincts.","She travels only at night.","She never speaks to strangers.","She hires a guard."],"a":0},{"q":"What advice do they give to nervous travellers?","o":["Start small, for example with a weekend somewhere close","Go on a long trip at once","Never travel alone","Take a group"],"a":0}],"ph":[{"q":"We're _____ to visit a small village in the mountains.","o":["planning","plan","planned","plans"],"a":0},{"q":"A big advantage _____ that it is cheap.","o":["is","are","has","does"],"a":0},{"q":"Solo travel is exciting. _____ the other hand, it can be lonely.","o":["On","In","At","By"],"a":0},{"q":"If I _____ in this situation, I'd call my family first.","o":["were","am","will be","would be"],"a":0}]},{"id":"u6","n":6,"t":"Goodness","a":["volunteer","charity","generous","donate","community","cause","selfless","fulfilling","spare","gratitude","willing","encourage"],"b":["waste","leftovers","edible","surplus","expiry date","redistribute","campaign","combat","packaging","portion","household","throw away"],"box":["volunteer","donate","cause","fulfilling","selfless","spare","community","charity","gratitude","generous"],"gap":[["Every summer she gives her time to ___ at an animal shelter.","volunteer"],["They decided to ___ their old furniture instead of selling it.","donate"],["Protecting the environment is a ___ worth supporting.","cause"],["Helping others is one of the most ___ things you can do.","fulfilling"],["It was a ___ act — she asked for nothing in return.","selfless"],["Can you ___ ten minutes to help me carry these?","spare"],["The whole ___ came together to rebuild the park.","community"],["He works for a ___ that provides food to families in need.","charity"],["The old man's eyes filled with ___ when we helped.","gratitude"],["Thank you — that was incredibly ___ of you.","generous"]],"rt":"Small Acts, Big Difference","rp":["We often imagine that changing the world requires great wealth, power or talent. Yet a growing body of research suggests that some of the most meaningful differences are made by ordinary people performing small, everyday acts of kindness. Whether cleaning a local park, choosing to donate unwanted clothes, or helping a stranger carry heavy bags, these actions add up — and, surprisingly, they benefit the giver just as much as the receiver.","Sofia, a nineteen-year-old student, discovered this by accident. Feeling lonely after moving to a new city, she signed up to volunteer at a centre for elderly people. She expected it to be a chore, but instead it changed her life. \"I made friends, I felt useful, and for the first time in months I wasn't thinking only about my own problems,\" she says. Volunteering provided a selfless and fulfilling sense of purpose. Sofia still volunteers every week, encouraging others with her simple lesson: \"You don't need to be rich or famous to make someone's day better. You just need to care, and to show up.\"","Mr Adeyemi, who runs a community charity, has watched hundreds of volunteers pass through his doors. He argues that kindness is a skill that grows with practice. \"When people first arrive, many are shy and unsure,\" he explains. \"But after a few weeks they become more confident and more generous.\" He believes that strong communities are built not by governments alone, but by neighbours who are willing to spare a little of their time.","Scientists who study human behaviour agree that kindness has powerful effects. Psychologists have found that helping others can reduce stress, lower feelings of loneliness, and even improve physical health. When we do something generous, the brain releases chemicals that cause a \"helper's high\", leaving volunteers with profound gratitude. One psychologist notes, \"Kindness feels good because, throughout human history, it has helped us survive.\"","Critics point out that a single donated coat will not end poverty, arguing that serious problems need serious solutions from those in power. Supporters respond that small actions and big changes are not enemies, as every successful campaign began with individuals acting for a good cause. Furthermore, a single kind gesture can spread through a community like a ripple across water, touching people the original giver will never even meet. Waiting for someone else to fix the world usually means nothing changes at all."],"rq":[{"q":"The main idea of the text is that …","o":["Only wealthy and powerful people have the ability to make a real difference.","Small acts of kindness bring positive benefits to both the helper and the receiver.","Volunteering in community projects is usually a disappointing and exhausting task.","Scientific research shows that helping others has no real impact on society."],"a":1},{"q":"What can we infer about Sofia when she first started volunteering?","o":["She was a confident person who easily made new friends in the city.","She decided to volunteer mainly to earn money.","She already had a lot of experience working with elderly people.","She was feeling isolated and did not expect the experience to be very rewarding."],"a":3},{"q":"The phrase \"helper's high\" refers to …","o":["the financial cost and time needed to participate in voluntary work.","a special medical treatment used by doctors to reduce stress levels.","the positive emotional and physical feeling people get from being generous","a dangerous activity that volunteers should avoid in their daily lives."],"a":2},{"q":"Which sentence best expresses what supporters say to the critics?","o":["They argue that personal actions are useless unless the government gets involved.","They point out that major positive changes often start with caring individuals.","They believe that only large international charities can fix social problems.","They suggest that ordinary citizens should wait for political leaders to act."],"a":1}],"who":{"p":["Sofia","Mr Adeyemi","Psychologist","Critics"],"q":[["Big social problems need serious solutions from powerful leaders, not just small individual actions.","Critics"],["Being kind is like a skill that improves the more you practice it in daily life.","Mr Adeyemi"],["You do not need money or fame to help others; you just need to care and be there for them.","Sofia"],["Helping others feels good today because cooperation was necessary for human survival in the past.","Psychologist"]]},"tf":[["Sofia decided to give up volunteering immediately after her very first experience at the elderly centre.","False"],["Mr Adeyemi considers kindness to be a practical skill that develops as people gain more experience.","True"],["Scientific research confirms that offering help to others can effectively decrease stress and social isolation.","True"],["Before managing the community charity, Mr Adeyemi spent several years working as a professional scientist.","Not Given"]],"lq":[{"q":"What is the programme mainly about?","o":["The process of setting up a café","The different ways in which people are trying to cut down on food waste","The origins and development of cooking","The various ways in which smartphones can be used"],"a":1},{"q":"According to the presenter, roughly how much of the world's food is wasted?","o":["about atenth","about a quarter","about a third","about a half"],"a":2},{"q":"How does Hannah feel about the time she spends donating food?","o":["She regards it as an excessive amount of work.","She considers it a minimal effort for a big result, and wishes she'd started sooner.","She feels that getting involved was ultimately a regrettable decision.","She finds it virtually impossible to fit into her routine."],"a":1},{"q":"On Diego's app, what do customers receive?","o":["A complimentary lesson in cooking","An order of food delivered directly to their homes","A restaurant meal for which they pay the full price","A \"surprise bag\" containing unsold food, available at a considerably lower price"],"a":3},{"q":"What is Diego's most important point?","o":["Most food waste comes from ordinary homes, so habits must change.","Apps could potentially eliminate the problem of food waste completely.","Shops are the primary, and essentially the only, source of food waste.","Technology is of little or no use in tackling the problem of food waste."],"a":0},{"q":"When Hannah says \"best before\" doesn't mean \"dangerous after\", she means:","o":["Food should be considered unsafe once it has passed its best-before date.","Food can often remain perfectly safe to eat even after its best-before date has passed.","Food should always be thrown away as soon as it reaches its best-before date.","The meaning of the label is too unclear for consumers to understand."],"a":1}],"lwho":{"p":["Hannah","Diego"],"q":[["Don't be too proud to buy \"ugly\" fruit and vegetables.","Diego"],["If we can't sell it, we donate it.","Hannah"],["Most food waste comes from ordinary households.","Diego"],["It only takes me ten extra minutes a day.","Hannah"],["Learn to love your leftovers.","Hannah"]]},"au":"audio/u6.mp3","rq2":[{"q":"Which of these is an example of a small act of kindness in the text?","o":["Building a hospital","Cleaning a local park","Starting a company","Winning a competition"],"a":1},{"q":"Why did Sofia sign up to volunteer?","o":["She needed money.","She felt lonely after moving to a new city.","Her school asked her to.","She wanted to become famous."],"a":1},{"q":"What does Mr Adeyemi believe about strong communities?","o":["Governments alone build them.","They are built by neighbours willing to spare some of their time.","They need a lot of money.","They are rare."],"a":1},{"q":"According to psychologists, helping others can:","o":["make people tired","reduce stress and loneliness","cost a lot of money","cause stress"],"a":1},{"q":"In the text, \"spare\" in \"spare a little of their time\" means:","o":["to keep for yourself","to give because you can","to waste","to borrow"],"a":1},{"q":"The phrase \"like a ripple across water\" suggests that kindness:","o":["disappears quickly","spreads and touches many people","needs a lot of water","happens only once"],"a":1}],"lq2":[{"q":"What did Hannah see every evening at closing time?","o":["Perfectly good food going into the rubbish","Customers arguing","Empty shelves","Delivery lorries"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Hannah do with the unsold food?","o":["She gives it to a local shelter.","She sells it cheaply the next day.","She takes it home.","She feeds it to animals."],"a":0},{"q":"How did Hannah feel about the rules at first?","o":["She worried about the rules, safety and time.","She didn't care.","She was angry.","She was excited."],"a":0},{"q":"What does Diego say about the number of meals his app has rescued in three years?","o":["Millions","A few hundred","Thousands","Exactly one million"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Diego say about his app?","o":["It will solve the problem alone.","It won't solve the problem alone; real change must happen in our homes.","It is only for restaurants.","It is too expensive."],"a":1},{"q":"What does Diego say about \"ugly\" fruit and vegetables?","o":["They taste worse.","A bent carrot tastes the same as a straight one.","They are dangerous.","They are cheaper to grow."],"a":1}],"ph":[{"q":"We _____ that many people throw away food.","o":["noticed","notice to","noticing","notices"],"a":0},{"q":"Our main goal _____ to reduce food waste.","o":["is","are","do","have"],"a":0},{"q":"To solve this problem, we _____ to start a campaign.","o":["want","wants","wanting","wanted of"],"a":0},{"q":"Volunteers can support each other _____ sharing tasks.","o":["by","at","in","for"],"a":0}]}];
